# msgpack (GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964) and setuptools (CVE-2025-47273) are flagged by Trivy
# at versions 1.1.2 / 70.3.0, but those versions come from pip's own internal
# vendor.txt manifest (/opt/venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip/_vendor/vendor.txt),
# which just records which versions pip vendors for its own bundled/internal use. This
# is not an installed application package and is not reachable via this connector's
# code or any untrusted external input.
#
# Verified 2026-09-18 in the built image (2026.1.x line, same pip-vendor pattern as
# the database-connector, email-connector, gitlab-connector, and minio-connector
# fixes, issue #2439).
#
# Re-evaluate if pip itself bumps its vendored copies to fixed versions, or if a
# future finding shows a real installed-package occurrence (not pip/_vendor).
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
CVE-2025-47273

# Flask==2.0.0 / Werkzeug==2.0.0 (CVE-2023-30861, CVE-2023-25577, CVE-2024-34069) are
# hard-pinned by scrapydweb==1.6.0's own requirements (Requires-Dist: Flask==2.0.0,
# Werkzeug==2.0.0). Bumping either in this project's requirements.txt has no effect:
# scrapydweb's own exact pin wins during its separate `pip install scrapydweb` step
# and reinstalls the old versions.
#
# TODO: real fix requires one of:
#   - forking/patching scrapydweb to relax its Flask/Werkzeug pins and verifying its
#     admin UI still works against Flask>=2.3/Werkzeug>=3.0
#   - dropping scrapydweb (build with WITH_SCRAPYDWEB=0) if the dashboard isn't needed
#   - accepting the risk (scrapydweb is an internal-network admin UI, not
#     internet-facing, in the current deployment)
# Re-evaluate if scrapydweb publishes a release with newer Flask/Werkzeug pins.
CVE-2023-30861
CVE-2023-25577
CVE-2024-34069
